e write her and capture  that spirit that tone something about it  how you know how funny and then all of a  sudden she turn on a dime and break your  heart and then my grandfather died  really suddenly very much like in the  film you know she was right at that  place where he could no longer really  take care of her and no longer really  deny that he couldn't take care of her  and he just died not quite as suddenly  as in the movie but he just passed away  I'm a broken heart I think and I was at  his funeral listening to the eulogy my  mom gave which was very similar to this  eulogy and just been like well this is a  movie someone here is making this movie  and then looking around the theater or  the theater the church in Illinois or  you know looking for who that person  would be it didn't occur to me then that  it would be me  I thought it was supposed to be somebody  else and then I supposed to like run up  to them and say oh you're making this  movie didn't you know and it wasn't  until six months later when I I thought  I don't think anyone's doing this but I  decided to write that draft that first  draft so well that's the story yeah  Robert if ever a character was suited to  an actor or vice-versa it is you and  Bert  and I'd like to know when you read the  script for the first time what did you  think and what did you feel you know I  have only seen this picture twice and  you've seen it a lot of times but I am  was struck by how good every actor in  this picture was start with Tyson  god she was good and so it's Blythe and  so was Hillary and Michael I am just  amazed at how good the actors  and the reason that they are one of the  reasons they are good two of the reasons  they are good is because you wrote a  wonderful movie and when I read it I  could hear it in my mind I could speak  those words they come from Chicago and  I'm from Rochester New York which is not  that far away it's along the Great Lakes  the the accent is very similar and and  because 